        "listenerPandoraId": "LI:1745910464",
        "description": "Radiohead had the potential of being an alt-rock one hit wonder in the early 1990s but became oh so much more. Their 1993 hit \"Creep\" fit in so perfectly with the buzz bin bands of that time, with its crunchy choruses and self-loathing lyrics, that few would have yeah predicted the impact Radiohead would have later in the career. With 1995's The Bends, Radiohead started their transition into an album oriented band with a set well-crafted and performed songs that critics began to take notice. However, it was 1997's OK Computer, with its ambitious song structures, dystopian lyrics, lush arrangements and grand scale, that would prove to be their breakthrough and turn Radiohead into the most important band of their era. Their hugely anticipated follow up album, Kid A, was released in 2000 and had the band continuing experimentation but this time embracing electronic music. Since then, Radiohead have continued to release multiple raved about albums that continue to push musical boundaries."
      },
